{
  "headline": "President Mahama arrives in Moscow for third Russia–Africa Summit",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "President John Dramani Mahama of Ghana arrived in Moscow on Monday to lead a delegation to the third Russia–Africa Summit. Hosted by Russian President Vladimir Putin and scheduled for October 28–29, 2026, the summit is expected to include more than 30 African heads of state and government. The forum is anticipated to focus on peace diplomacy, security cooperation, and equitable economic partnerships. Upon arrival, the President was met by Russian government officials and Ghana’s Ambassador to Russia, Dr. Koma Steem Jehu-Appiah, along with embassy staff. Mahama traveled to Moscow from Ethiopia prior to the summit's commencement. During the visit, the President is expected to participate in plenary sessions, an Economic and Humanitarian Forum, and a kente exhibition organized by the Russia Ghana Parliamentary Forum. He is also scheduled to grant audience to various Russian state institutions and businesses. Additionally, the Russian University of Transport and the Peoples’ Friendship University of Russia are set to confer honorary doctorate degrees upon him during separate ceremonies."
    },
    {
      "text": "Speaking at the Financial Times Africa Summit on October 21, President Mahama stated he intends to use the Moscow visit to press for a ceasefire in the four-year-old Russia–Ukraine war. He framed this objective within Ghana's non-aligned foreign policy, describing the nation as having cordial relations with the United States, the European Union, China, and Russia. Foreign Affairs Minister Samuel Okudzeto Ablakwa confirmed that President Mahama is expected to hold a high-level bilateral meeting with President Putin on the margins of the summit to deepen relations between the two countries."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva wins Brazilian presidential runoff as Flávio Bolsonaro seeks annulment",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Brazilian President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva secured a fourth nonconsecutive term on October 25, 2026, defeating far-right Senator Flávio Bolsonaro in a presidential runoff. Lula received 51.79% of the valid votes, totaling approximately 64.26 million ballots, while Bolsonaro received 48.21%, or 59.83 million votes. This result represents a margin of roughly 4.4 million votes and a reversal from the October 4 first round, in which Bolsonaro led Lula by approximately 2.2 million votes. Lula is the first candidate since Brazil's return to democracy in 1985 to win a presidential runoff after finishing second in the opening round. Following the announcement, Lula vowed that dictatorship and fascism would not return to Brazil while addressing supporters in São Paulo. The victory was met with celebrations in Rio de Janeiro and São Paulo, where supporters gathered in the streets. Global figures, including Mexican President Claudia Sheinbaum, Canadian Governor General Mary Simon, and U.S. Senator Bernie Sanders, offered congratulations, while U.S. Congressman Ro Khanna called for President Donald Trump to respect the results. Conversely, Donald Trump Jr. criticized the outcome regarding a voting extension. Senator Flávio Bolsonaro, the son of former president Jair Bolsonaro, refused to concede and called for the election to be annulled. Bolsonaro described the decision to extend voting hours as an “unacceptable” and “dirty trick.” The extension, ordered by Supreme Court Justice Cristiano Zanin, was requested by both candidates due to heavy rains and reports that buses had been pulled from circulation, which Lula claimed barred citizens from voting. However, the president of the electoral court, Justice Kassio Nunes Marques, stated the extension did not affect the result, and leaders of the supreme court and congress have recognized the re-election. An analysis by the newspaper Folha de S.Paulo indicated that only 34,330 votes, or 0.03% of the total, were cast after the original 5 p.m. closing time, a figure described as insignificant compared to the 4.4 million-vote margin."
    }
  ]
}

