  "headline": "Bank of Canada faces conflicting economic indicators",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Canadian economy is currently experiencing two distinct issues that present a challenge for the Bank of Canada. According to CBC Canada, inflation recently rose to 3.1 per cent, though price increases have so far been contained to the energy sector while core measures remained subdued. While inflation is rising, the economy is also characterized by weak growth, and the impact of the latest tranche of tariffs has not yet been measured. These competing factors create a dilemma for the central bank: weak growth typically prompts interest rate cuts to spur activity, whereas rising inflation often prompts rate increases to slow the economy and control prices."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Ghana Climate Literacy Portal seeks to expand resources for young Africans",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Ghana Climate Literacy Portal (GCLP) is working to provide young Africans with knowledge and resources to respond to climate change. The digital platform is being highlighted at the Regional Conference of Youth Africa (RCOY Africa) in Accra, where advocates are discussing environmental solutions and ways to increase youth participation in climate policy. Since its launch last year, the portal has attracted more than 5,000 learners, according to Mary-Ann Essilfie-Arhin, the leader of the GCLP. Essilfie-Arhin stated the platform is designed to connect climate science, policy, advocacy, and practical solutions through digital learning. The GCLP team is currently looking to expand course offerings and introduce language tools to reach French- and Swahili-speaking communities to address language barriers in accessing educational resources."
    },
    {
      "text": "At the RCOY Africa conference, Saustine Lusanzu, a Tanzanian climate advocate and founder of the Earth Care Foundation, described the portal as an opportunity for African youth to build the knowledge necessary to lead environmental change. Lusanzu stated that access to reliable climate learning materials could assist young people in moving from awareness to taking practical action within their communities. The RCOY Africa conference serves as a platform for young leaders to develop proposals and contribute to a shared youth statement regarding climate policy and decision-making priorities."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Student-led initiative targets low voter turnout in Peel region, Canada",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "A student-led initiative is working to address barriers to civic engagement in an effort to reverse low voter turnout trends in the Peel region ahead of Ontario's municipal election on October 19, 2026. The community group, Voice the Vote, was co-launched by the Democratic Engagement Exchange (DEE) at Toronto Metropolitan University and United Way Greater Toronto. Through outreach, regular events, and educational tools, the group aims to increase attention to local politics in the Peel, York, and Toronto regions. Abby Tait, the group's senior public affairs manager, stated that the initiative aims to center community voices to drive conversations about elections following provincial and federal snap elections. Voice the Vote utilizes a 'vote pop-up toolkit' featuring a mock ballot and voting screen to engage people with municipal issues before directing them to candidate information resources."
    },
    {
      "text": "Data from the Association of Municipalities of Ontario (AMO) shows that the 2022 municipal election reached a record-low provincial average voter turnout of 32.9 per cent, down from approximately 38 per cent in 2018. In the Peel region, 2022 turnout rates were lower than the provincial average: 24.5 per cent in Brampton, 31.7 per cent in Caledon, and just under 22 per cent in Mississauga. Members of Voice the Vote have identified a lack of awareness regarding the role of municipal governments and difficulties in accessing reliable, accessible information about candidates and platforms as factors contributing to lower voter numbers."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Police block cultural activists’ protest in Dhaka, Bangladesh",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Police prevented cultural activists from holding a planned human chain protest in the Dhanmondi area of Dhaka, Bangladesh, on October 26, 2026. The activists, organized under the banner of “Protesting Artistes and People,” had gathered in front of Meena Bazar at Dhanmondi 27 around 4:00 pm to protest what they described as attacks and aggression by individuals acting in the name of the Tawhidi Janata against Baul music and other cultural events in various parts of the country. The protest specifically aimed to address the obstruction of female Baul singers from performing in Sunamganj. Prior to the gathering, more than 100 police personnel had taken positions in the area starting at 3:30 pm."
    },
    {
      "text": "During the encounter, theatre activist Sumana Islam approached the police to ask if the human chain would be permitted. According to reports, police stated they had received instructions from higher authorities to ensure participants did not chant provocative slogans or make provocative remarks. When police noted that a previous group at the same location had chanted the slogan “Joy Bangla,” Dipak Shil, a former president of the Bangladesh Chhatra Union, questioned if the slogan had become spoiled, noting it was used during the July uprising. Following an argument, police detained Shil and placed him in a vehicle. Sumana Islam, who attempted to stop the detention, alleged that officers confronted her aggressively and manhandled her."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "New executive committee elected for BMTA in Bangladesh",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Bangladesh Medical Tourism Association (BMTA) has elected a new executive committee for a one-year term. According to a statement reported by The Financial Express Bangladesh, the election activities were led by Election Commissioner Dr. Mohammad Jamshed Alam and took place on Sunday at a city hotel. Khandaker Sitabur Rahman, the founder and CEO of Khandaker Medical Tourism, was elected General Secretary of the association."
    },
    {
      "text": "The newly elected committee includes Vice Presidents Shabbir Ahmad Tamim, co-founder and CEO of MediAider, and AMR Mojumder Liton, CEO of WCA Medical Evacuation Service. Ainul Tareq Nobel was elected Joint Secretary (Finance), and Md. Abdullah Al Atiq was elected Joint Secretary (Admin). Other executive members of the committee are Nazmul Islam Rafi, Abdur Rashid, Daud Arman, Mohammad Ariful Islam, S. Arif Hossain, and Gias Mohammad Azizul Haque."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Syrian Interior Ministry announces arrests of Yahya al-Assad and former officials in Homs",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "On October 25, 2026, Syria’s Interior Ministry announced the arrest of Yahya al-Assad, a cousin of former Syrian President Bashar al-Assad, during operations in the city of Homs. The arrests were conducted by the Internal Security Command and included several former officers and officials. Interior Ministry spokesperson Nour al-Din al-Baba stated that those detained included the aforementioned individuals."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Bank of Ghana researchers develop AI-powered inflation tracking system",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Researchers at the Bank of Ghana (BoG) have developed an Artificial Intelligence (AI)-powered inflation tracking system called e-Inflation, which is capable of providing near real-time estimates of consumer price movements, according to a study. The system uses Big Data and AI techniques to monitor prices across online platforms to generate inflation estimates before official figures are released. Currently, official inflation data in Ghana are compiled through conventional price collection methods and are typically published after the end of each reference month. The study noted that while delays are necessary for data validation and compilation, they can limit the ability of policymakers and market participants to respond quickly to changing economic conditions. The e-Inflation model relies on machine learning algorithms and web-scraping tools to collect and analyze large volumes of price data from websites and other digital sources, tracking goods and services frequently purchased by consumers."
    },
    {
      "text": "The study found that the e-Inflation measure closely tracked Ghana’s official inflation rate during the study period, demonstrating an ability to mirror official trends. Researchers suggested that these AI-driven estimates could serve as a reliable complement to traditional statistical methods and provide early signals of changes in the cost of living. The researchers stated the model could assist policymakers, businesses, and investors in accessing timely information to improve economic decision-making. Specifically, the study noted that real-time indicators could be useful for the Bank of Ghana’s Monetary Policy Committee, which makes interest rate decisions based on current and expected inflation developments. The study also highlighted that the increasing use of online platforms by consumers and businesses creates opportunities to collect price information at a higher frequency and lower cost."
    }
  ]
}
